PHASE TRANSITION UNIVERSALITY ANALYSIS
============================================================

Tournament phase transitions:
----------------------------------------
  beta_1 onset              n= 3  arcs=  3  dark_frac=0.556  desc: First non-trivial homology
  beta_3 onset              n= 6  arcs= 15  dark_frac=0.556  desc: First 3-holes appear
  beta_3 > 1                n= 8  arcs= 28  dark_frac=0.547  desc: Multiple 3-holes; seesaw breaks
  beta_4 onset              n= 8  arcs= 28  dark_frac=0.547  desc: 4-dimensional holes appear
  Real roots fail           n= 9  arcs= 36  dark_frac=0.543  desc: I(Omega,x) can have complex roots
  Claw-free fails           n= 9  arcs= 36  dark_frac=0.543  desc: Omega not claw-free
  S_{2,1,1}-free fails      n=10  arcs= 45  dark_frac=0.540  desc: Omega not S_{2,1,1}-free

Napolitano phase transition:
  Layer 16 of 24 = 0.667 = 2/3
  For 2-norm arch: ~65% depth
  For 1-norm arch: ~83% depth

2/3 ratios in tournament theory:
----------------------------------------
  n= 3: transitivity of regular = 0.0000 (c3=1, total=1)
  n= 5: transitivity of regular = 0.5000 (c3=5, total=10)
  n= 7: transitivity of regular = 0.6000 (c3=14, total=35)
  n= 9: transitivity of regular = 0.6429 (c3=30, total=84)
  n=11: transitivity of regular = 0.6667 (c3=55, total=165)
  n=13: transitivity of regular = 0.6818 (c3=91, total=286)

  Formula: transitivity = 1 - (n-1)(n-3)/(4(n-2))
  As n->inf: transitivity -> 3/4 = 0.75
  At n=7: transitivity = 6*4/(4*5) = 24/20 = ... 
    n=3: formula = 1.0000
    n=5: formula = 0.3333
    n=7: formula = -0.2000
    n=9: formula = -0.7143
    n=11: formula = -1.2222


Score variance ratio:
----------------------------------------
  n= 3: avg_var/max_var = 0.7546 (avg_var=0.503, max_var=0.667)
  n= 5: avg_var/max_var = 0.4977 (avg_var=0.995, max_var=2.000)
  n= 7: avg_var/max_var = 0.3762 (avg_var=1.505, max_var=4.000)
  n= 9: avg_var/max_var = 0.3008 (avg_var=2.005, max_var=6.667)
  n=11: avg_var/max_var = 0.2505 (avg_var=2.505, max_var=10.000)


Active energy fraction for trained vs random:
----------------------------------------
Random attention (our computation):
  n= 4: active=0.132, dark=0.683, dark/(active+dark)=0.838
  n= 7: active=0.187, dark=0.723, dark/(active+dark)=0.795
  n=11: active=0.221, dark=0.727, dark/(active+dark)=0.767
  n=16: active=0.245, dark=0.723, dark/(active+dark)=0.747

For n=4 (Napolitano): dark/(anti+dark) = 0.838
This means 84% of non-scalar energy is 'dark' for random attention.
Napolitano's 'discovery' that dark carries information is partly
explained by this baseline imbalance.


Conclusion: The '2/3' is NOT a universal constant.
It appears in Napolitano because 16/24 = 2/3 for Qwen-0.5B.
It does NOT appear at a fixed fraction in tournament homology.
The universal ratio, if any, is (n+1)/(n-1) for dark/active dims.
